WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.760
If you haven't had a chance to watch El Camino
on Netflix yet, make a U-turn and queue it

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:05.280
up.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.300
If you have seen it, carry on as we dissect
the ending and figure out what it all meant.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:10.880
Major spoilers ahead!

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:15.540
It's no exaggeration to say that Jesse Pinkman
went through a lot during the original series.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:20.180
He was chained up, extorted, held hostage
by a fly, and beaten within an inch of his

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:24.040
life, and to top it all off, he was present
while two of his girlfriends died.

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:26.080
Through it all, this was a guy who was on
the run.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:30.200
Sometimes that was literal, sometimes more
figurative, as he ran from his choices in

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:30.700
life.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:33.580
Think about the first and last times we ever
laid eyes on Jesse.

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:36.700
The first time we ever saw him in action,
he was running from the cops.

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:40.780
The last time, he was speeding into the night,
running away from the neo-Nazi compound.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:43.080
He's framed by the chase from beginning to
end.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:45.540
"Quit running and get some help."

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:47.780
"No one is trying to hurt you, son."

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:51.520
And in El Camino, Jesse spends a good chunk
of time on the run as well.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:56.140
He's basically public enemy number one, as
we see when a caravan of police cars screams

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:59.100
onto the scene at the first sniff of Jesse's
whereabouts.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:04.120
By the end of it all, he finally finds a place
to stop running, and maybe, just maybe, finds

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:05.080
a little peace.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:09.240
Just before the title card cuts in, we see
a hint of a smile pass over his lips.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:13.640
Whatever comes next for Jesse, he'll be forging
his own path through a brand new frontier.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:18.520
Breaking Bad's body count is ridiculously
high, and many of those corpses were added

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:20.440
to the pile by Walter White himself.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:24.100
For his part, Jesse Pinkman has surprisingly
little blood on his hands.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:28.720
Over the course of five seasons, he only directly
caused the deaths of three people: cartel

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.940
enforcer Joaquin Salamanca, naive coffee lover
Gale Boetticher, and closet psychopath Todd

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:34.920
Alquist.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:37.260
Every one of those deaths ate him up inside.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:40.000
Well, except maybe Todd's, but Jesse is no
killer.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.750
He'd rather get arrested than shoot a police
officer, and there's a point where he can't

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:46.990
even bring himself to shoot Todd after everything
the guy put him through.

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:50.740
But by the climactic showdown in the film,
Jesse is ready to do whatever it takes for

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:51.580
his freedom.

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:55.860
It's a darker edge to the absolution he wants,
but it shows that he's done being a prisoner

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:56.620
or a pawn.

00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:58.700
"You ready?"

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:02.500
"Yeah."

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.180
It's possible that he doesn't realize it right
away, but the flashback with Jane suggests

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:09.160
that he does come to an understanding about
it.

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:13.579
They talk about going where the universe takes
you, and Jesse is on board with the philosophy

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:15.560
until Jane brings him back down to Earth.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.260
"I've gone where the universe takes me my
entire life.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:24.000
It's better to make those decisions for yourself."

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:28.600
It's no accident that the flashback occurs
just before the final shot of Jesse driving

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:29.100
away.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:33.220
He's finally taken control of his life and
made those decisions for himself.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:36.980
The cat's out of the bag; Walter White is
back in El Camino.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:41.460
But there's no miraculous return to life here;
it's just a flashback to a conversation Jesse

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.540
and Walt had in a New Mexico diner.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:47.540
It appears to take place during the events
of the Season 2 episode that saw them trapped

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:51.960
in the desert, just before Jesse dropped Walt
off at the airport, judging by their clothes.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:56.200
The conversation is seemingly innocuous and,
let's be honest, it's mainly a way to get

00:02:56.209 --> 00:03:00.579
Bryan Cranston back into the role for one
last hurrah, but it also suggests hints for

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:01.880
Jesse's new future.

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:05.300
Walt urges him to think about going back to
college, then says:

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:08.500
"You're really lucky, you know?

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:12.500
You didn't have to wait your whole life to
do something special."

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.980
Is Jesse finally doing something that he sees
as special?

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:20.959
Although Walter was probably referring to
things like making piles of money and becoming

00:03:20.959 --> 00:03:25.450
a respected drug kingpin, Jesse may see putting
his regrets and mistakes in the past as a

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:28.220
more personal measure of what makes something
special.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:33.419
Before Jesse parts ways with Ed in Alaska,
he pulls an envelope from his pocket and asks

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:35.220
Ed to mail for him as one last favor.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:39.120
Inside is a handwritten letter, and when Jesse
drives away we see that it's addressed to

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:43.020
Brock Cantillo, the son of his girlfriend
Andrea, whom Todd shot.

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:44.100
What does the letter say?

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:49.380
The film never shows us, but most likely it's
an apology to Brock, and maybe even an explanation

00:03:49.389 --> 00:03:52.769
so he doesn't spend his whole life living
with the question mark of his mother's death.

00:03:52.769 --> 00:03:57.389
In the first scene of the film, Mike Ehrmantraut
tells Jesse that while he may be able to start

00:03:57.389 --> 00:04:00.180
fresh, the one thing he can never do is make
things right.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:04.340
And maybe a letter from his mom's boyfriend
won't change Brock's life, but to Jesse, it's

00:04:04.349 --> 00:04:05.389
important to try.

00:04:05.389 --> 00:04:09.129
He won't bring back Brock's mom with the letter,
but it may at least bring a smidgen of closure

00:04:09.129 --> 00:04:13.560
to a tragedy which, in Brock's eyes, came
out of nowhere and had no apparent cause.

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:17.640
The folks behind Breaking Bad have often described
how the clothing on the show is designed to

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:19.660
clue viewers into a character's mental state.

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:23.880
For example, Walter White starts out wearing
beige, but his clothes get darker the more

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:25.140
he transforms into Heisenberg.

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:27.140
Showrunner Vince Gilligan told Vulture:

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:30.860
"Color is important on Breaking Bad; we always
try to think in terms of it.

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:34.430
We always try to think of the color that a
character is dressed in, in the sense that

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:36.659
it represents on some level their state of
mind."

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:39.500
That trend continued all the way to the end
of El Camino.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:42.720
When Jesse steps out of the shower, he's dressed
himself in all black.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:46.880
He even gets a last-minute gift from Pete,
a black beanie, completing the look.

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:50.520
But when he drives off on his own in Alaska,
he's donned a white sweater.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:55.220
The wardrobe shift takes Jesse from a character
consumed by inner turmoil into a man who's

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:58.120
driving a single road into a brighter, cleaner
future.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:01.420
Even the flashback scene with Walt in the
diner pays attention to this.

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:05.360
Earlier in their partnership, Jesse was still
clinging to the good inside him, plenty of

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:06.520
white in his outfit.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:09.640
Conflicted, sure, but not yet giving in to
anger, loss, and despair.

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:14.280
It took Jesse awhile to find the right clothes
again, but he's finally leaving some of that

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:15.280
darkness behind.

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:19.960
In spite of all the positive connotations,
there's a bitterness to the ending of El Camino

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:23.360
that's hard to deny: Jesse is truly, completely
alone.

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:26.060
"Anyone else you want to say goodbye to?"

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:32.200
When he ventures into obscurity in Alaska,
he does so with only memories of the people

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:33.220
he once cared about.

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:35.940
That's not to say that viewers weren't warned
that it would come to this.

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:40.240
Prior to El Camino's premiere, Aaron Paul
tweeted a scene from season 3 of the series,

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:41.360
along with the caption:

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:43.820
"Cats out of the bag...and the bag is in the
river.

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:47.140
Here’s a moment from Breaking Bad to slowly
prepare you all for what’s to come."

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:51.460
In that scene, Jesse is at his emotional wits
end and flat-out turns down Walter's offer

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:52.780
to keep working together.

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:55.520
Jesse tells him, with heartbreaking intensity,

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.600
"I have never been more alone.

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:01.840
I have nothing.

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:04.080
No one."

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:07.280
Unfortunately, that hasn't changed by the
ending of El Camino.

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:09.540
If anything, he's even more alone than ever.

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:10.920
Was it all worth it in the end?

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:12.480
Hopefully, the answer is...

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:15.820
"Yeaaah, b----!"

